# The two readings that are about the link rather than about the node.
#
# node-trend.scene shows the chart opened over a *sensor* reading - what the box in the field is
# measuring. This is the other half of the same screen: what the client measured about the box.
#
# Every other trend on a node comes off a Telemetry packet the node chose to send. These two are
# taken by the radio in your hand, off the header of whatever arrived, which is why they answer
# the question somebody standing outside with an antenna is actually asking - not "what is it
# reporting" but "is this link getting better or worse as I walk".
#
# The scene is arranged around the rule that makes them safe to draw at all: a ratio is only
# this node's own when the packet crossed the air to us directly. `signal` sets that, and a node
# reached through a relay or over a bridge has neither bar nor line - the figure stays on the
# row, because it is true, it is just not about what the label says.

scene demo
scale 4
delay 220

# ---- open the node first ----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The Nodes list has three rows before its first node - the filter row, the sort row and the Map
# (MESH_UI_NODES_LEAD_ROWS) - so Echo Repeater is seeds[5] and row 8, exactly as node-trend.scene
# counts it.
#
# It is opened *before* the packets arrive, and that ordering is the point rather than a
# convenience. The list is ranked by last heard, and an arrival is the freshest thing there is -
# so every press of `signal` below would move this node up the list under the cursor. The detail
# does not care: it is remembered by node id rather than by row, which is the whole reason it is
# (see `node_detail_node` on struct mesh_ui_nav). A scene that counted rows afterwards would be
# counting a list that had reordered itself seven times.
tab nodes
key down 8
key a
hold 400

# ---- a walk away from the repeater -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# Seven packets, each one a little further out: the ratio falls through the amber threshold and
# the strength falls with it. They move together here, which is the ordinary case and not the
# only one - a loud band with a loud noise floor is a fine RSSI and a dead link, which is the
# whole reason the two are banded on different questions and drawn on two axes.
signal ECHO -2 -71
hold 220
signal ECHO -4 -78
hold 220
signal ECHO -6 -84
hold 220
signal ECHO -8 -89
hold 220
signal ECHO -11 -97
hold 220
signal ECHO -13 -104
hold 220
signal ECHO -15 -110
hold 400

# ---- the rows, and then the pictures ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# Right steps by *group* rather than by row, which is both how this screen is meant to be crossed
# and what keeps the scene from breaking the next time a reading is added: two presses is
# Actions, Identity, Signal however many rows those turn out to be. It lands on the group's first
# stop, which is the SNR row - because a fact is not a stop, and the ratio is the first thing in
# the group that A does anything to.
#
# The bar under each reading is what earns it that press, and it is there only because the packet
# came straight from the node - the same condition the trend behind it was kept on.
key right 2
hold 1200
key a
hold 2600

# ---- and the other one ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# B lands back on the row the chart was opened from rather than on the list, because a chart is a
# level of the detail. One row on is received strength, and it is its own place rather than a
# second line on the first: dB and dBm are two verticals, and a plot carries one.
key b
key down 1
key a
hold 2600
key b
hold 700
